# Caddy reverse-proxy for the two eu-reg services.
#
# Regulatory information, not legal advice.
#
# TOPOLOGY (one minimal reference, provider-neutral)
# ------------------------------------------------
# Two independent FastAPI processes over one shared read-only snapshot, each on
# its own origin behind a single TLS-terminating reverse proxy. Separate
# hostnames keep the scan-to-check service worker (scope "/" on its origin)
# from colliding with the eu-reg-api playground's /static routes:
#
#   https://api.EXAMPLE          -> eu-reg-api  (label checker + playground)
#   https://scan.EXAMPLE         -> scan-to-check (barcode -> checker + PWA)
#
#   api.EXAMPLE/ , /formula , /health , /v1/... , /static/...
#   scan.EXAMPLE/ , /sw.js , /manifest.webmanifest , /api/... , /static/...
#
# BOTH services bind to 127.0.0.1; only the proxy is exposed. TLS is terminated
# here (Caddy issues/renews certificates from a CA automatically for a public
# domain). Everything behind the proxy is loopback-only.
#
# DO NOT expose the two service ports (8000/8010) publicly; only the proxy
# listens on 80/443 to the public interface.
#
# Rate limiting: Caddy's built-in build has NO `rate_limit` directive (that is
# a third-party plugin). The V1 scope has no accounts, so the bounded,
# no-accounts rate limit is applied at the APPLICATION layer (an in-memory,
# per-IP/per-origin limiter over the read endpoints, returning 429 + Retry-After
# and excluding /health and static assets). See docs/rate-limiting.md and the
# limit configuration in the proxy/container runbook. If you prefer proxy-level
# rate limiting, use nginx's built-in limit_req on the same two locations, or a
# Caddy build with the `ratelimit` plugin -- but do not add a second competing
# limit. Keep ONE documented limiter.
#
# LOCAL VALIDATION (loopback, no public exposure, no machine trust change):
#   See the local TLS section in deploy/linux/README.md for generating a
#   temporary CA + certs and pointing Caddy at them so both origins, the
#   scanner service-worker scope and the error semantics can be exercised over
#   HTTPS without altering OS/device cert trust. The proxy itself always binds
#   to the loopback interface for the local validation run. Use
#   deploy/linux/local-Caddyfile for that run.

{
	# Bind the proxy to loopback ONLY during local validation. On a real host you
	# enable the public listener (0.0.0.0) behind your firewall/cloud security
	# group; a public Caddy listener is NOT used here.
	admin off
}

# --- api origin -------------------------------------------------------------
api.EXAMPLE {
	encode zstd gzip

	# Explicit routing (all paths to the eu-reg-api process).
	handle {
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8000
	}

	# Request body / payload limit for the API (the form/check endpoints).
	request_body {
		max_size 64MiB
	}
}

# --- scan origin ------------------------------------------------------------
scan.EXAMPLE {
	encode zstd gzip

	handle {
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8010
	}

	# The scan service's PWA shell + OCR are served alongside /api/; allow the
	# OCR vendor tree (~27 MiB) plus a healthy margin.
	request_body {
		max_size 80MiB
	}
}

# HTTP -> HTTPS redirect (leave the public app at HTTP until TLS is trusted).
http://*.EXAMPLE {
	redir https://{host}{uri} 308
}
